(N'182', N'youtube', N'<i>‘The sun enables life. The rain grants it safe passage’.</i><br><i>WINTER RAIN</i><br><i>The winter sky is a widow&#39;s sky, bedarkened and weeping. The clouds are chulish and kraken-cruel. They release great torrents of water and burst balloons of moisture. It pours like the floods described in the Bible, flooding rivers, drowning fields and overflowing dams. It is a deluge of rain like Noah&#39;s Ark, an endless stream of water pouring from the sky. Trees are uprooted, cars are tossed about and whole villages have disappeared in a froth of foam. Cities are submerged and people live in fear of the unknown, with power outages. The rain is relentless. It crackles and crackles like bracken pods in a bushfire. It seems the floodgates in the sky have opened and there is no one to close them. Is this a scene from a science-fiction film? Is this a horrifying vision of a future world? It really isn&#39;t. This is the new reality for people from Missouri to Manchester, Mumbai to Melbourne.</i><br><i>SPRING SKY</i><br><i>The spring sky is delicate, pellucid-blue. The clouds are fragile and angel-white. They drift with the light, gusty breeze. Mother Earth&#39;s soil is as hard as titanium and needs nourishment. The misty rain falls. It is as fragile as a Scottish smear and its misty dew feels like warm butter melting on the face. As it falls, it opens the glassy fingers of winter&#39;s frosty fist, one by one. The flowers in the meadows bloom slowly and wave like the arms of coral at low tide. The rivers exhale with a murmur of satisfaction. The spring rains have come and they are as sinless and sparkling as an angel&#39;s tears.</i><br><i>SUMMER RAIN</i><br><i>The summer sky is neon-blue and vibrant. The sun-crisped flowers of the meadow are wilting. They look up at the tufted clouds and beg another dose of insulin for their parched petals. The clouds oblige and rain descends in little gleam-drops of silver. If you were to stand in the meadow, the drops would feel as sparkly and effervescent as champagne bubbles hitting your skin. The sound of the rain is a harmonic thrumming, nature’s white noise. Silver trickles of water seep into the soil, renewing the life-roots of the plants beneath. A homely, baked-earth smell rises from the land as it is washed and cleansed by the dewy tears of summer rain. Petrichor, the smell of the first rains after a dry spell, rises like a miasma. It is a jasmine-and-gingerbread fragrance, warm and fresh, and it laves the land with sweetness.</i>', N'0', NULL),
